 [:db/cas 17592186172639 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = (x - 1)(y + 2)$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. At every point $(x, y)$ where $x > 1$ and $y > - 2$, the slope of the slope field is positive\n2. At every point $(x, y)$ where $x < 1$ and $y > - 2$, the slope of the slope field is positive\n3. At every point $(x, y)$ where $x > 1$ and $y < - 2$, the slope of the slope field is positive" "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = (x - 1)(y + 2)$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** At every point $(x, y)$ where $x > 1$ and $y > - 2$, the slope of the slope field is positive\n- **II.** At every point $(x, y)$ where $x < 1$ and $y > - 2$, the slope of the slope field is positive\n- **III.** At every point $(x, y)$ where $x > 1$ and $y < - 2$, the slope of the slope field is positive"]
 {:db/id 17592186172639 :question/answer-fields ["field-q-96475/authored-checkbox-v1/I" "field-q-96475/authored-checkbox-v1/II" "field-q-96475/authored-checkbox-v1/III"] :db/ensure :question/validate}
 [:db/cas 17592186172648 :question/worked-solution nil "For xy^2, y^2 is positive in every open quadrant, so the slope has the sign of x. It is negative in quadrants II and III, and positive in quadrant I."]
